      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "International organizations like the UN are fundamentally designed to prioritize global consensus over the absolute sovereignty of any single nation. Their structures are inherently weak because they rely on the voluntary compliance and political will of powerful member states, which invariably leads to gridlock. Major powers can effectively veto or dilute resolutions that threaten their core national interests, rendering the organization toothless when true conflicts of sovereignty arise. Therefore, these bodies are bureaucratic forums, not enforceable protectors of individual state interests.",
      "source": "target",
